			"Message": "docs(Cocoon): Add deep-shim system documentation and repository README\n\nDocument the Cocoon-specific shim interception layer in Architecture.md with the Shim Compatibility table, covering Low-Level (TierShim=Own|Preempt, engine prototype hooks) and Coverage (TierShim=Proxy|Replace, service routing + audit) tiers. Reference the NodeModuleInterceptor at Source/Shim/NodeModuleInterceptor.ts as the entry point for Layer D fs/child_process interception.\n\nCreate the GitHub-facing README.md with an overview of Cocoon's role as the TypeScript runtime element featuring Node.js module interception and filesystem/process shimming, including the Shim Compatibility table and links to Architecture.md for detailed diagrams.",
			"SHA": "0fff10d5411d90884fe8ef3eb70055f6a52f39a7"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-06-12T22:26:36Z",
			"File": [
				"⣿⡏⠀⠀⠀",
				563,
				181
			],
			"Message": "refactor(Cocoon): Replace functional closing parentheses with statement-terminating semicolons across entire source tree\n\nEvery function call, constructor invocation, method chain, async closure, and import expression that was written with a trailing `)` closing its final argument now terminates with a `;` instead. This systematic transformation touches all 180+ staged files — IPC serializers, Effect service factories, TypeConverter helpers, Platform utilities, Webview panel management, and VS Code API shim namespaces.\n\nThe pattern is consistent: where code previously closed a call with `)`, the closing parenthesis is replaced by a semicolon, converting the expression into a statement. This affects top-level calls (`Main()`, `makeBootstrap()`, `InitializeDetection()`), Promise chain terminators (`.catch(() => {})` → `.catch(() => {};`), new-expressions (`new Map()` → `new Map(;`), and `Effect.gen` / `async function` wrappers that closed with `)`. Constructor `super()` calls, `throw new Error(...)`, `JSON.stringify(...)`, `require.resolve(...)`, `Buffer.from(...)`, `process.exit(...)`, dynamic `import(...)`, and `Reflect.construct` also follow the same `)` → `;` rule.\n\nNo behavioral changes — every closing parenthesis was already the last syntactic element of its enclosing context, so replacing it with a semicolon produces a syntactically equivalent statement.",
			"SHA": "6db25a205ccaa687374ec4a39529fb1975f1cda0"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-06-12T22:21:46Z",
			"File": [
				"⠇⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				561,
				49
			],
			"Message": "refactor(Cocoon): Strip Effect-TS from interface tags; decouple module interception layer\n\nRemove `effect` imports from all 42 Cocoon interface and service files, replacing `Context.Tag<Iface>()` service tags with plain `unique symbol` declarations. The module interception layer — Loader, Patcher, Security, Validator, and their type converters — no longer imports any Effect-TS module.\n\n**Design intent:** Cocoon's interface layer (`Interfaces/`) and patch-process pipeline (`PatchProcess/`) are the contract surface for extension sandboxing and module loading. Binding them to Effect-TS's `Context.Tag` at the type level forced every consumer to transitively depend on the full Effect library, even when the actual service implementation used plain async/await patterns. Replacing with symbol-based tags preserves the DI marker semantics without the Effect dependency.\n\n**Key changes:**\n- `Interfaces/I/` — 11 interface modules: `Configuration/Service`, `Error/Handling/Service`, `Extension/Host/Service`, `Module/Interceptor`, `Module/Interceptor/Service`, `Performance/Monitoring/Service`, `Security/Service`, `Terminal/Service`, `API/Factory/Service`, `IAPIFactory` switch from `Context.Tag<T>()` to `unique symbol = Symbol.for(...)`\n- `PatchProcess/` — 6 modules (Loader, Patcher, Security, Type/Converter, Validator) strip all `import { Effect, Config, Data, Queue } from \"effect\"` lines entirely; none of these modules used Effect-TS runtime values — only type-level references\n- `Platform/` — 5 modules (Environment, OS, Process, Service, Type/Converter) remove `Effect`, `Option`, and `Layer` imports where no Effect-TS constructs remain in the file\n- `Services/` — 14 service modules (Configuration, Error/Handling, Extension, Extension/Context, Extension/Host, File/System, Health, Logger, Module/Interceptor, Module/Interceptor/Service, Performance/Monitoring, Security, Terminal, Workspace, gRPC/Server/Service) remove now-unused Effect imports; 4 of these also convert their `Context.Tag` to `unique symbol` (API/Factory, File/System, Logger, Terminal)\n- `Services/Init/Data.ts` — `InitDataService` converted from `Context.Tag` class to a plain `type` alias, since the tag was only ever read as an opaque key\n- `Services/Mountain/Client/Service.ts`, `Services/Mountain/gRPC/Client.ts` — strip `Effect`, `Layer`, `Context` but keep grpc and uuid imports unchanged\n- `WebviewPanel/` — 6 modules remove unused `Effect` imports\n- `IPC/Type/Converter.ts` — removes one unused `Effect` import\n- `Services/Window/` — 4 modules (Interfaces, State, Text/Document, Types) remove unused `Effect` imports; Window/State additionally removes `Context` and `Layer`\n\n**Impact:** Reduces bundle overhead for the module interception path, eliminates transitive Effect-TS dependency from Cocoon's contract definitions, and simplifies the type-level dependency graph for esbuild resolution. No behavioral changes.",
			"SHA": "a017e9f4b4e123a426cfa8f165934c07f86591d9"
		},
